  "turning_point_description": "Hamza Ali Mazari crosses from Afghanistan into Pakistan via the Torkham border crossing. He carries documents establishing him as a Baloch from Quetta with criminal connections. In Lyari, he takes work as a waiter at Mohammed Aalam's juice shop \u2014 his cover employer and field handler. He must establish himself as a credible, low-level Lyari resident without triggering the neighbourhood's intense communal surveillance, while beginning to map the gang structure from the ground up.",
  "real_world_correlation": {
    "primary_inspiration": "Ravindra Kaushik's Pakistan insertion (mid-1970s) via similar indirect route. RAW's classic deep-cover insertion route avoids direct India-Pakistan border crossing (too surveilled) in favour of third-country routing (Nepal, Afghanistan, Gulf states).",
    "torkham_route": "Torkham (Khyber Pass) crossing is a real high-traffic commercial border point. Afghan/Central Asian refugee flows in 2001-2002 made it operationally feasible for an individual to blend in without standing out as an Indian national.",
    "juice_shop_cover": "Handler using a small commercial business (juice shop, tea stall, shop) as cover is standard tradecraft \u2014 provides plausible reason for cash transactions, regular community contact, and a meeting point that doesn't look like a dead drop.",
    "lyari_ground_truth": "Lyari is a real Karachi neighbourhood, historically controlled by Baloch-majority PAC (Peoples' Aman Committee). Outsiders are noticed but Baloch from Quetta/Balochistan province are common \u2014 consistent cover for a Punjabi-origin operative given intensive Balochi dialect training."
  },
